            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Soup Loving Nicole"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I could only find rye already sliced so I just made a regular sandwich. I agree that the tongue needed a little more seasoning than what the recipe calls for. Add a little of whatever you like and use when making a beef roast and you can't go wrong.",
            "datePublished": "2015-02-04T18:02:05.427Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Fedr"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It is much easier to peel off the outer skin if you put the cooked beef tongue from boiling boullion directly into ice cold water for 3-5 minutes. \n\nAfter that temperature shock the outer skin removes without knife? only with the help of your fingers.",
            "datePublished": "2014-03-14T03:54:22.277Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Eskian"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I liked this, needed some more flavor for the tongue, so I add a couple minced garlic cloves and a couple of bay leafs to the cooking part. The peppercorns mentioned by previous rater sounds good, too. Removing the tongue from the liquids after it is cooked is a must. Cool it off in the refrigerator to help make slicing easier.",
            "datePublished": "2011-01-25T01:10:34.227Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CJ McD"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It needs a flavor boost. I would recommend adding a couple cloves of garlic, some pickling spice and a tablespoon or two of red wine or cider vinegar to the simmering liquid. Make sure you let the tongue cool in the liquid.\n\nIt's a nice basic starter recipe, so it gets three stars.",
            "datePublished": "2008-02-03T16:30:31.967Z"
          },
          {
